+\paragraph{Does the fine-tune undo what the pretraining holds? (2026-10-29)}\label{par:ft_harm} Neither round above
+explains why a fine-tune loses to scratch on some probes at small $D$, and the user asked whether fine-tuning actively
+hurts the pretrained model there. This reads the saved validation curves of every fine-tune that ends above scratch's
+best: the $142$ of the grid's $840$ fine-tune cells with a negative gain (\code{data/nogain.json}, each cell's best trial, all
+twelve probes and $D$), and the losing runs of the two small-$D$ rounds, $15$ default, $35$ linear probe then
+fine-tune, $43$ L2-SP and $30$ frozen-block runs of $420$ (the first round's curves copied into
+\code{data/lowd\_ft\_curves.json} before its run directories are deleted). Per run, in MSE of $\log|\mathcal M|^2$: scratch's best
+$L_{\rm scratch}$, the run's best checkpoint, and its start, the validation loss at step $0$ (no arm resets the head:
+the pretrained network as is, from the read-out job at the cell's own $D$, else the zero-shot score of
+\code{zero\_shot.json}, taken with the probe's $D=10^4$ target statistics, within $0.49$ decades of the cell's own where
+both exist; for linear probe then fine-tune, the read-out head it starts from). A losing run (i) starts above
+$L_{\rm scratch}$ and improves on its start without reaching it, (ii) starts at or below $L_{\rm scratch}$ and ends above
+it, (iii) starts above and ends at or above its start. The same three classes are read a second time with the frozen
+trunk's ridge read-out in place of the start, where one was fitted (rungs $1$, $7$, $9$ at $D\le 10^{2.5}$ on the five
+small-$D$ probes, $ee\to Z\gamma$ at $10^{1.5}$ and $10^2$ for every rung, $u\bar u\to Zg$ for rungs $6$ and $7$): the
+read-out is a model the same $D$ events give from the frozen pretraining, so a fine-tune ending above it has lost
+what the trunk already carried (\code{analysis/transfer/ft\_harm.py}, \code{data/ft\_harm.json}; Fig.~\ref{fig:ft_harm}).
